{"cefr": "B2", "topic": "economics", "body": "Before the 1950s, loading a cargo ship was slow, dangerous work. Goods arrived at the docks in barrels, sacks and crates of every size, and teams of labourers moved each item by hand. A vessel might spend longer in port than at sea, and theft was so routine that it was simply priced into the cost of shipping. The change came from a trucking company owner rather than from the shipping industry itself. His insight was not the metal box, which already existed, but the idea that the box should never be opened between origin and destination. Cranes could lift a sealed container from a lorry directly onto a ship, and the contents would not be handled again until they arrived. Ports resisted. The new system needed different cranes, deeper berths and far fewer dockworkers, and the unions understood immediately what that meant. Several major harbours delayed adoption for a decade and lost their traffic permanently to rivals that had not. The economic effect is hard to overstate. Shipping costs fell so far that where a factory stood mattered much less than it once had, and manufacturing began to move across oceans in ways that would have made no sense before.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is mainly concerned with", "correct_answer": "how a change in handling cargo reshaped where goods could profitably be made", "distractors": ["the working conditions of dockworkers in the early twentieth century", "the engineering problems involved in designing a metal shipping container", "a dispute between trucking companies and shipping lines over freight rates"], "explanation": "The passage moves from the old loading system to the container to its effect on where factories could sit, ending on manufacturing moving across oceans. Dockworkers and the box itself appear in service of that argument rather than as the subject, and no freight-rate dispute is described.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "According to the passage, theft at the docks was", "correct_answer": "common enough to be treated as a normal cost of shipping", "distractors": ["eliminated once containers were introduced", "the main reason ports resisted the new system", "rare because goods were counted by hand"], "explanation": "The passage says theft was so routine it was priced into the cost of shipping. It never claims containers ended it, and the resistance is attributed to cranes, berths and jobs rather than to theft.", "difficulty_est": 3}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The passage suggests that the ports which delayed adoption", "correct_answer": "misjudged how quickly shippers would move to harbours that had converted", "distractors": ["lacked the deep water needed for the new vessels", "were prevented by law from installing the new cranes", "recovered their traffic once they eventually invested"], "explanation": "Traffic was lost 'permanently' to rivals that had adopted, which only makes sense if the delaying ports underestimated how fast shippers would leave. The passage gives no legal obstacle, and 'permanently' rules out recovery.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the passage, \"insight\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "a realisation that others had missed", "distractors": ["a financial investment", "a technical drawing", "an official permission"], "explanation": "The word introduces an idea explicitly contrasted with the metal box that already existed, so it names something understood rather than something built or bought. None of the other options can be 'not the box, but the idea'.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author mentions that the metal box already existed in order to", "correct_answer": "locate the real innovation in how the box was used rather than in the object", "distractors": ["credit the shipping industry with the original invention", "explain why the containers were made of metal rather than wood", "suggest that the trucking company owner made no real contribution"], "explanation": "The clause exists to set up the contrast in the same sentence: not the box, but the rule that it stays sealed. The passage credits the outsider rather than the industry, and never discusses materials.", "difficulty_est": 5}]}
{"cefr": "B2", "topic": "science", "body": "Antibiotics work by exploiting differences between bacterial cells and our own. A drug that blocks the construction of a bacterial cell wall, for instance, harms the bacterium and leaves human tissue untouched, because our cells build no such wall. The difficulty is that bacteria reproduce with extraordinary speed. A population can double in twenty minutes, and each division carries a small chance of a copying error. Most errors are harmless or fatal to the cell, but occasionally one produces a bacterium that survives a drug which kills the rest. That survivor then has an entire environment to itself. This is ordinary evolution, running fast enough to watch. What makes it a medical problem rather than a curiosity is the scale on which antibiotics are used. Every course prescribed, and every dose given to farm animals to speed their growth, applies the same pressure to whichever bacteria are present. Developing new antibiotics has proved unexpectedly difficult, and the commercial incentive is weak: a drug taken for a week earns far less than one taken for life. Several large companies have closed their antibiotic programmes entirely, leaving much of the remaining work to universities and small firms.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is primarily about", "correct_answer": "why resistance develops so readily and why replacing the drugs has proved hard", "distractors": ["the chemical structure of the first antibiotics", "the case for banning all antibiotic use in farming", "how bacterial cells differ from human cells"], "explanation": "The passage explains the mechanism of resistance, then turns to the weak incentive to develop replacements. The cell-wall difference and farm use are steps in that argument, and no ban is advocated anywhere.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "According to the passage, a bacterial population can double in", "correct_answer": "twenty minutes", "distractors": ["twenty hours", "a single day", "about a week"], "explanation": "The figure is stated directly. The week appears elsewhere, describing how long a course of antibiotics is taken, which is a different measurement entirely.", "difficulty_est": 2}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The passage suggests that a resistant bacterium multiplies quickly because", "correct_answer": "the drug has removed the competitors it would otherwise have had", "distractors": ["it reproduces faster than non-resistant bacteria do", "it can pass its resistance to human cells", "farm animals carry it between different locations"], "explanation": "'That survivor then has an entire environment to itself' means the drug cleared the competition rather than that the survivor is inherently faster. Nothing suggests resistance transfers to human cells.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the passage, \"pressure\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "a force determining which organisms survive", "distractors": ["physical weight on a surface", "urgency felt by doctors", "demand from patients for treatment"], "explanation": "The word describes what every prescription applies to whatever bacteria are present, in a paragraph about evolution — it names selection, not weight or urgency. The passage never mentions what doctors or patients feel.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author contrasts a drug taken for a week with one taken for life in order to", "correct_answer": "explain why companies have little reason to develop antibiotics", "distractors": ["argue that courses of antibiotics should last longer", "show that antibiotics work faster than other medicines", "criticise the price charged for long-term medication"], "explanation": "The contrast appears immediately after 'the commercial incentive is weak' and explains it: a short course earns less. The passage recommends nothing about course length and makes no complaint about pricing.", "difficulty_est": 4}]}
{"cefr": "C1", "topic": "society", "body": "When a local newspaper closes, the effects reach further than the loss of its reporting. Studies of American towns that lost their paper found that municipal borrowing costs rose afterwards, because lenders had less independent information about how a town was run and priced that uncertainty into their loans. Turnout in local elections fell as well, and the number of candidates standing for office declined. Where nobody reports on a council, fewer people know what it does, and fewer still see a reason to contest a seat on it. National outlets have not filled the gap. Their economics depend on reaching very large audiences, which makes a planning dispute in a town of thirty thousand impossible to justify covering. Online alternatives have appeared in some places, but they tend to concentrate where advertising money already is, which is rarely where the closures happen. Some researchers argue the losses are best understood as a public problem rather than a commercial one, since the benefits of local reporting fall largely on people who never bought the paper. That framing points towards subsidy or foundation funding, neither of which sits comfortably with a press expected to hold power to account.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is mainly concerned with", "correct_answer": "the wider civic costs of local newspaper closures and why nothing has replaced them", "distractors": ["a decline in the quality of national newspapers", "how lenders decide the price of municipal bonds", "the history of advertising revenue in the print industry"], "explanation": "The passage catalogues effects beyond lost reporting, then explains why neither national nor online outlets fill the gap. Bond pricing is one piece of evidence rather than the subject, and national quality is never discussed.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "According to the passage, borrowing costs rose in affected towns because lenders", "correct_answer": "had less independent information about how the town was governed", "distractors": ["knew the towns had lost advertising revenue", "were charged more by national news outlets", "saw that fewer candidates were standing for office"], "explanation": "The passage states the reason directly: less independent information, priced in as uncertainty. The fall in candidates is listed as a separate effect, not as the cause of the borrowing costs.", "difficulty_est": 3}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The passage suggests that online local outlets have not solved the problem because", "correct_answer": "they appear in the places least likely to have lost a newspaper", "distractors": ["readers are unwilling to trust reporting published online", "they are barred from attending council meetings", "their journalists have less experience than those in print"], "explanation": "They concentrate where advertising money already is, 'which is rarely where the closures happen' — so they emerge in the wrong places. The passage raises no question of trust, access or experience.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the last paragraph, \"framing\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "a way of understanding the issue", "distractors": ["a wooden structure", "a false accusation", "a decorative border"], "explanation": "The word refers back to treating the losses as a public rather than commercial problem, and what follows from that view. The other meanings of the word are literal or legal and make no sense of 'points towards subsidy'.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author ends by noting that subsidy sits uncomfortably with the press in order to", "correct_answer": "acknowledge a difficulty in the solution just proposed", "distractors": ["argue that local newspapers are not worth saving", "praise foundations for the funding they already provide", "explain why national outlets refuse to accept public money"], "explanation": "The sentence immediately follows the proposal of subsidy or foundation funding and qualifies it: a press funded this way is meant to hold power to account. It concedes a problem rather than rejecting the whole idea.", "difficulty_est": 5}]}
{"cefr": "B2", "topic": "psychology", "body": "For most of the twentieth century, sleep was treated as a period in which the brain simply rested. That view has been abandoned. Recordings taken during sleep show patterns of activity as complex as those in waking, and much of it appears to be concerned with the previous day. The clearest evidence comes from experiments in which volunteers learn a task and are then either allowed to sleep or kept awake. Those who sleep perform better the following day, and the improvement is larger than the difference in tiredness can explain. In some studies the sleeping group improves on a skill they did not practise again at all. Exactly what happens is still contested. One account holds that the brain replays the day's sequences at speed, strengthening the connections involved. Another emphasises the opposite process: weakening connections that were not useful, so that the important ones stand out by comparison. The two accounts are not incompatible, and evidence exists for both. What has changed is the underlying assumption. Sleep is no longer regarded as time subtracted from learning, but as a stage of it.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is primarily concerned with", "correct_answer": "how sleep came to be understood as part of learning rather than a pause in it", "distractors": ["the medical consequences of long-term sleep deprivation", "a disagreement about how many hours of sleep adults require", "techniques for recording brain activity during sleep"], "explanation": "The passage opens with the old view, presents the evidence that displaced it, and closes on sleep as a stage of learning. Recording techniques are mentioned once as evidence, and neither deprivation nor sleep duration is discussed.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "In the experiments described, volunteers who were allowed to sleep", "correct_answer": "performed better the following day than those kept awake", "distractors": ["reported feeling less tired but performed the same", "needed to practise the task a second time", "learned the task more quickly at the outset"], "explanation": "The passage states that sleepers perform better the next day. It explicitly says the gain is larger than tiredness explains, and that in some studies no further practice occurred.", "difficulty_est": 3}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The detail that some volunteers improved without practising again suggests that", "correct_answer": "the gain came from sleep itself rather than from additional rehearsal", "distractors": ["the task was too easy to require any practice", "tiredness had no effect at all on performance", "the waking group had been practising incorrectly"], "explanation": "Removing further practice removes the obvious alternative explanation, leaving sleep as the source of the improvement. The passage says tiredness explains part of the difference, just not all of it.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the passage, \"contested\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "still disputed among researchers", "distractors": ["measured repeatedly", "difficult to observe directly", "discovered only recently"], "explanation": "The word introduces two competing accounts of the same process, so it marks disagreement rather than difficulty or novelty. The passage then presents both sides, which is what a contested question looks like.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author notes that the two accounts are not incompatible in order to", "correct_answer": "stop the disagreement being read as one theory defeating the other", "distractors": ["argue that neither explanation has evidence behind it", "show that the second account has replaced the first", "suggest that the question has now been settled"], "explanation": "The sentence follows two opposed-sounding explanations and says evidence exists for both, heading off the assumption that one must win. The next sentences make clear the settled part is the assumption about sleep, not the mechanism.", "difficulty_est": 5}]}
{"cefr": "B2", "topic": "history", "body": "Before 1840, sending a letter in Britain was expensive and paid for by the person who received it. Charges depended on distance and on the number of sheets, which meant that every letter had to be measured and assessed by hand. Recipients who could not pay, or did not wish to, simply refused delivery, and the Post Office absorbed the cost of carrying the letter both ways. The reform proposed a single low charge for any letter under a certain weight, anywhere in the country, paid in advance by the sender. Its author argued that most of the cost lay in handling rather than in transport, so distance should not affect the price at all. Officials objected that revenue would collapse. It did fall at first, and did not recover for years. But the volume of letters rose so sharply that the reform was never seriously reversed, and other countries copied it within a decade. The adhesive stamp itself was almost an afterthought, a practical way to show that postage had been paid. It became the most visible part of a change that was really about pricing.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is mainly about", "correct_answer": "a change in how postage was priced, of which the stamp was only the visible sign", "distractors": ["the invention and design of the first adhesive stamps", "the financial troubles of the British Post Office in the 1840s", "how other countries built their own postal services"], "explanation": "The passage describes the old pricing, the proposed replacement, the objection and the outcome, closing by saying the change was really about pricing. The stamp gets one sentence, and other countries a clause.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "Before the reform, the cost of sending a letter depended on", "correct_answer": "the distance travelled and the number of sheets", "distractors": ["its weight alone", "whether the sender had paid in advance", "the value of whatever was described inside"], "explanation": "Both factors are stated directly. Weight belongs to the reform rather than the old system, and advance payment by the sender is precisely what the reform introduced.", "difficulty_est": 3}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The passage suggests that refused deliveries were costly because", "correct_answer": "the letter had already been carried and nothing was collected for it", "distractors": ["recipients were fined for turning letters away", "refused letters had to be stored indefinitely", "senders demanded compensation for the delay"], "explanation": "The Post Office 'absorbed the cost of carrying the letter both ways', which only makes sense if the work was already done and unpaid. No fines, storage or compensation appear anywhere.", "difficulty_est": 5}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the passage, \"afterthought\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "something added late and of secondary importance", "distractors": ["a widely admired invention", "a decision taken in haste", "an idea borrowed from another country"], "explanation": "The word is set against the pricing reform as the thing that actually mattered, and the next sentence calls the stamp merely the most visible part. It marks the stamp as minor, not as hasty or foreign.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author mentions that revenue did fall at first primarily to", "correct_answer": "concede that the officials' objection was partly justified", "distractors": ["explain why the reform was eventually reversed", "criticise the reform's author for poor forecasting", "show that other countries were right to wait"], "explanation": "The admission sits directly after the officials' objection and directly before 'But the volume of letters rose' — a concession before the counter-argument. The passage says the reform was never reversed and that other countries copied it quickly.", "difficulty_est": 5}]}
